// c-data-map-10: a script whose block fuses whole loses its `type`
// definitions, and the shell prints its result raw.
//
// command: timeout -s KILL 5 graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/c-data-map-10.gx
//          (it never exits: an exit statement is an effect, keeps the block
//          from fusing whole, and the result then prints correctly)
//
// expected: {inner: {col: 1}, n: 2}, as --no-fusion prints.
//
// observed (HEAD c722befe, debug build; no cache, cold and warm alike):
//   [["inner", [["col", 1]]], ["n", 2]]
// and with --log-dir and RUST_LOG=warn the log says
//   error, type { inner: C, n: i64 } does not match value ...
//
// The whole file block fuses into one kernel; fusion::fuse
// (graphix-compiler/src/fusion/mod.rs:978) discards the replaced block, and
// deleting it runs TypeDef::delete (node/mod.rs:570), which undefines C and
// S. The root type the shell captured (graphix-rt/src/gx.rs:916) still names
// C through a resolution cell that holds its definition weakly, so TVal's
// is_a_with fails and Display falls back to the naked form
// (graphix-types/src/typ/tval.rs:277). The same types declared in a module
// (`mod m;`, `let s: m::S = ..`), a flat `type P = {x: i64, y: i64}`, or a
// `println("x");` statement in the file all print correctly.
type C = {col: i64};
type S = {inner: C, n: i64};
let s: S = {inner: {col: 1}, n: 2};
s
